{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/751049-turtle-mokole-not-that-far-fetched-after-all","id":751049,"title":"Turtle Mokole: Not that far fetched after all","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-03T03:50:27-08:00","id":751049,"title":"Turtle Mokole: Not that far fetched after all","content":"I remember during the CB kickstart there was debate over how related turtles were to other Mokole kin, I just found this study I thought was interesting:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2014/11/141124103225.htm\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/images/scidaily-icon.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2014/11/141124103225.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> Turtles and dinosaurs: Scientists solve reptile mysteries with landmark study on the evolution of turtles </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2014/11/141124103225.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2014/11/141124103225.htm </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> A team of scientists has reconstructed a detailed &amp;apos;tree of life&amp;apos; for turtles. Next generation sequencing technologies have generated unprecedented amounts of genetic information for a thrilling new look at turtles&amp;apos; evolutionary history. Scientists place turtles in the newly named group &amp;apos;Archelosauria&amp;apos; with their closest relatives: birds, crocodiles, and dinosaurs. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-03T04:39:36-08:00","id":751149,"title":"","content":"A giant fire breathing turtle....now where have I heard that before....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-03T07:32:39-08:00","id":751399,"title":"","content":"It wasn't nearly so great an issue for the Mokole, since they already had members making a jump from Archosauria to Lepidosauria, and they helped Garou, placental mammals on both fronts, to assume the shape of a marsupial that was vaguely kind of like a wolf in appearance. For us, though, it is interesting. I suppose that we're going to find out how those numerous unresolved polytomies and other ambiguous aspects of reptile phylogeny square out somewhat sooner than expected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-05T15:25:20-08:00","id":757390,"title":"","content":"I thought that turtle Mokole used to be a thing, but they went extinct somehow and remain extinct for some story purpose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6384-frozenfishy","name":"Frozenfishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T03:38:16-08:00","id":758344,"title":"","content":"They are, it's just during the CB Kickstart there was a debate from the backers on if they should be included in the book. One person insisted the idea of turtle mokol\u00e8 was stupid because they thought turtles were too far removed from croc and lizards. Others said it shouldn't matter due to the bunyip and the divide between placental and marsupial mammals. This study basically tosses the taxonomic reason put the window.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T06:14:24-08:00","id":758517,"title":"","content":"Beast court turtle mokole of teenage years you say?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T19:06:48-08:00","id":759635,"title":"","content":"I admit I was skeptical about the whole turtle Mokole idea... but I think it was well-done.<br /> <br /> It's <i><b>still</b></i> awfully bizarre. Monitor lizards and crocodiles are apparently not any closer in scientific taxonomy, but they are similar in other ways: long-bodied, long-tailed, terrestrial or semiaquatic (or in the Mesozoic, fully marine) predators with fearsome bites that can move quickly either in the water, or on land. Turtles are herbivores, slow-moving, shelled, short-bodied, mostly toothless... in short, they don't fit the same mold.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Mokole claim that Nagah and Corax are descended from Mokole themselves, so it's possible to (re?)interpret the wereturtles as a Mokole offshoot that may have become a separate Changing-Breed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T22:46:00-08:00","id":759763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Erinys</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/751049-turtle-mokole-not-that-far-fetched-after-all?p=759635#post759635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I admit I was skeptical about the whole turtle Mokole idea... but I think it was well-done.<br /> <br /> It's <i><b>still</b></i> awfully bizarre. Monitor lizards and crocodiles are apparently not any closer in scientific taxonomy, but they are similar in other ways: long-bodied, long-tailed, terrestrial or semiaquatic (or in the Mesozoic, fully marine) predators with fearsome bites that can move quickly either in the water, or on land. Turtles are herbivores, slow-moving, shelled, short-bodied, mostly toothless... in short, they don't fit the same mold.</div> </div> </div> </div>Turtles are not all herbivores, many species are omnivores who do actively hunt, not just scavenge. Heck youtube Turtle vs Pigeon at some point. I mean sure they aren't strict carnivores like crocodilians or monitors, but turtles are actually closer in scientific taxonomy to crocodilians according to recent studies then lizards are. To me the divide between lizard and crocodilians made the Mokole come off as &quot;reptile&quot; more than having any set limitation to their kin species. This is even more extreme when dinosaurs are included (not even counting the bird kings and pouch kings which always bugged me).<br /> <br /> I did think that turtles should be involved in some way however, when you consider symbolically how often turtles are seen as beings of wisdom whose age allows them to span generations and house a great deal of information. To me that makes the turtle a better fit for &quot;memory&quot; than other species.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","user":"CeltSPZ","date":". 11-07-2015, 02:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-07T07:16:06-08:00","id":759971,"title":"","content":"Yeah I'm not saying I'm against it, just that I find it weird. Point taken about turtles having omnivory, and associations with wisdom and memory.<br /> <br /> As for taxonomy, I never cared when it comes to the Mokole as long as their animal forms are cold-blooded reptiles. (I assume the Bird Kings and Pouch Kings were entirely separate Breeds, the way Nagah and Corax are.) As you said, they clearly have an ability to breed with whatever, it's even written in their Gifts! (The Rokea and Ananasi, the other Breeds from the Mesozoic, also span large numbers of species. Theme and feel trumps scientific classification when it comes to the three eldest Breeds, IMO.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></